WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.520
- [Instructor] What we're
gonna do in this video

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.930
is try to think of the chain of events

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:08.390
that would happen if the
supply of loanable funds

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:10.400
were to increase in the United States.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.110
And the way that that could happen is

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.310
let's say the Federal Reserve were to

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.090
so to speak print money,
and then use that money

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.490
to buy treasuries in the US.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.210
So it's inserting those
Federal Reserve notes

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.560
into the quantity of loanable funds.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.660
Well what would happen is that the supply

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.740
of loanable funds would
shift to the right.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.200
So our new supply would look like this,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.550
would look like this.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.990
I'll call that S prime.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:43.100
And then we'd have a new
equilibrium price of those funds,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.580
which we would call
our real interest rate.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.920
So then we get to r prime.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.310
So our real interest rates have gone down,

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:56.210
and we have a higher quantity of money

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.940
that is being loaned, Q prime.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.090
But what would be the effect of that?

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.420
What would be the effect of that

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.250
relative to other countries?

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.640
And I'm just picking Japan
here as another country,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.190
but this could be the case
with many other countries

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.120
where they have relatively free flows

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.750
of goods and financial capital.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.060
And to help us think through that,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.500
I drew the balance of
payments for each country.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.960
And the balance of payments is made up of

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.180
the current account, which
is talking about the flows

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.810
of goods and services, and then you have

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.600
what's sometimes called a capital account,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.280
sometimes the capital
and financial account,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.350
which is talking about the flow of,

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.010
it oftentimes, you could think
of it as financial investment

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:40.050
or investments of some
kind or the flow of funds.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.563
So pause this video and think
about what would happen.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:50.140
Well, if the real interest rate
go down in the United States

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.130
and we're assuming that all else equal

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.160
in every other country, well
then you have a situation

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.190
where in Japan, the
relative real interest rates

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:04.190
are now higher, so relative,
relative real interest rates,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:13.343
interest rates, are higher.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.700
Now in general, people
might want to say hey,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.730
if I can get a higher
or a higher than before

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.750
relative real interest
rate, it might not be

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.640
absolutely higher, but it's
higher than it was before

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.380
relative to the United States,
well that might increase

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.630
the financial flows from
the United States to Japan.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.170
And so you might have some more people,

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.750
not everyone, but some
more people than before,

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.797
who want to take their
dollars, convert it into yen,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.560
and buy financial assets in Japan

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.010
where they can get that relatively now

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.440
higher real interest rate.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.080
Well if these folks are
converting from dollars to yen,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.310
what's the immediate effect of that?

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.750
Well, it will increase demand for the yen

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.830
and so the price of the yen
in dollar terms will go up.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.720
Or another way to think about it is

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.330
this is going to cause
the dollar to depreciate,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:18.330
depreciate, relative, relative to the yen.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.450
So we're just gonna put
that aside right over here,

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:24.730
'cause this is gonna
have other implications.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.510
But that dollar, it's used to buy yen,

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.291
and then those investors will
maybe buy Japanese bonds.

00:03:32.291 --> 00:03:34.260
And so what's happening?

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.710
Well we're talking about the
transfer of financial assets.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.450
And so in the United States,

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:44.330
the capital and financial
account, that will go down.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.780
This will go down.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.793
You could think of it as being debited.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.960
And in Japan, they're getting,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.190
they're getting an increase
in financial assets.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.210
People are investing
more in Japanese bonds.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.510
However you want to think about it.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.650
And so that increase
in funds, that goes up,

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.950
and so this is getting credited.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:07.350
Now, in other videos, we've
talked about how over time,

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.200
the balance of payments
tend to balance out.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.610
If one side is getting debited,

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.750
the other side is getting
credited, or vice versa.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.110
So how is that going to
work out in this situation?

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.800
Well that all goes back to
the fact that the dollar

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.100
has depreciated relative to the yen.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.200
If the dollar depreciates
relative to the yen,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.810
what is that going to do?

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.503
Well now American goods, American goods,

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:38.040
are relatively cheaper,
relatively cheaper,

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.040
or cheaper than they were
before, cheaper in Japan.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.100
That's hard to read.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:50.833
And Japanese goods more expensive,

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.760
more expensive, in the US.

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.310
And so what is going to happen?

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.970
Well in that situation,
that means that the US

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.700
is going to export more to Japan.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.290
Their goods are now relative,
are now cheaper in Japan,

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:12.580
Japanese goods are now
more expensive in the US.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:17.580
So they're going to buy fewer
Japanese, Japanese goods

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:28.010
and sell, and export, export more

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:32.960
American goods, American goods.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.480
And so what's going to happen
on our current account?

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.070
Well if your exporting more,

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.190
that means your current account goes up.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.913
It is going to be credited.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.280
It is going to be credited.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.780
And then the opposite's
going to happen to Japan.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.210
Relative to the US, it's going to export

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:52.030
less and import more.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:55.480
So its current account is going to be,

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.870
is going to be debited.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.240
Now economies are complex things,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.050
and what I've just done is a
little bit of a simplification.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:07.770
But these are the general
trends that you would expect.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.400
Other things that you
might expect is well,

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.400
if you have this flow of
financial capital into Japan,

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.940
well that might increase
their loanable funds.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.100
And so their real interest
rate might eventually go down

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.290
and all of these cycles would keep going

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.890
and reverberating back
and forth over time.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.760
But this is the general chain of events

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:35.160
that you might expect, that
the interest rates in Japan

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.890
will become relatively higher.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.940
And so you have a flow of financial funds

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.550
going from the US to Japan.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:46.410
In the process, when they
convert from dollar to yen,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:47.980
the dollar is going to get cheaper,

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.190
the yen's going to get more expensive.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.760
The American capital and
financial account goes down

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.600
'cause you have this net
outflow of financial funds.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.100
But because of the
depreciation of the dollar,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:03.491
the US is now importing
less and exporting more.

00:07:03.491 --> 00:07:07.340
Now a question is is this good
or bad for either country?

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.590
Well, it depends what
the country's goals are.

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.060
This might be good for
the US if their goal

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.100
was to export more American goods.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.860
Or it might be bad for
the US if they said hey,

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.620
now Japanese goods are more expensive,

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.355
and maybe they're dependent
on some type of Japanese goods

00:07:22.355 --> 00:07:24.380
in some way, shape, or form.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:25.750
That might not be the case with the US,

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.660
but in another country,
let's say they're dependent

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.680
on oil from other countries
or they're dependent

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:32.930
on military hardware from other countries.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.990
And if those things become
more expensive, or food,

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.010
that might make it a lot
harder for their citizens.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.000
So it's an interesting
thing to think about,

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.730
whether this is good or bad and how

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.380
all of these things fit together.

